@X0E
Many problems that our tech support department encounters are path problems.
These problems are often avoided if the sysop sticks to the default paths.
Of course, some deviation may be necessary, and that's why PCBoard is as
flexible as it is.  This document will cover the default paths and batch files,
and help you gain an understanding of what is necessary and what can be
changed.

@X0BNODE1.BAT / BOARD.BAT
---------------------

@X03The default files that PCBoard's install program creates for you is the
node1.bat, node2.bat and board.bat.  The function of the NODE?.BAT files is
simply to set environment variables.  These variables are primarily used only
for these batch files, so they may vary, depending on how your batch file is
written.
 
All node?.bat files (node1.bat, node2.bat, node3.bat, etc.) will "CALL" the
board.bat when they are finished.  The board.bat is shared between all nodes.
The advantage to this is low maintenance.  If you want to make a change, you
only have to update ONE batch file.
 
Since the BOARD.BAT is shared, the information in it is global.  The NODE
batch files have all the node-specific information in them, and that's why it
is necessary to have one for each node.

Examples are:


@X0BNODE1.BAT:
----------
@X07SET PCBNODE=1
SET PCBPORT=1
SET PCBDRIVE=C:
SET PCBDIR=\PCB\NODE%PCBNODE%
SET PCBDAT=%PCBDRIVE%%PCBDIR%\PCBOARD.DAT
SET PCB=/NODE:%PCBNODE% /COM%PCBPORT%:
SET DSZLOG=PCBDSZ.LOG
BOARD.BAT


@X0BBOARD.BAT:
----------
@X07IF EXIST PCBOARD.SYS DEL PCBOARD.SYS
:TOP
%PCBDRIVE%
CD %PCBDIR%
IF EXIST REMOTE.BAT REN REMOTE.SYS
IF EXIST EVENT.BAT DEL EVENT.BAT
IF EXIST DOOR.BAT DEL DOOR.BAT
PCBOARDM.EXE /FILE:%PCBDAT%
IF EXIST REMOTE.BAT CALL REMOTE.BAT
IF EXIST EVENT.BAT CALL EVENT.BAT
IF EXIST DOOR.BAT CALL DOOR.BAT
IF NOT EXIST ENDPCB GOTO TOP
:END


@X03As you can see, the SET statements in the node?.bat file set the node number,
com port number, current directory, etc.  It is necessary to give each node
it's own subdirectory.  This is because PCBoard will create files in the
current directory which are node-specific.  These files shouldn't be read by
other nodes, or problems would arise.  Among these are the EVENT.BAT, DOOR.BAT,
REMOTE.BAT, and PCBOARD.SYS.  Looking at the BOARD.BAT, you see why they must
have their own subdirectory, and it must be the current directory.

Let's look at the NODE1.BAT above, line-by-line.  The first two lines mean
nothing to PCBoard, but can easily be changed once, and affect all lines which
refer to them in the batch file.  PCBDRIVE and PCBDIR are referred to by the
BOARD.BAT (with the @X07%pcbdrive% @X03and @X07CD %pcbdir%@X03).  The board.bat could be
changed to say :
@X07
C:
CD \PCB\NODE%PCBNODE%
 
@X03That wouldn't be a problem.
 
The 6th line is very important.  The SET PCB= line sets the %pcb% environment
variable.  The information on this line overrides all settings in the PCBSETUP
program.  The node number, com port or fossil port, baud rate, IRQ, base
address, and several other switches can be specified here.  This line can be
left blank, but it is necessary to put these parameters on that line to share
a pcboard.dat file.

